                - img [ref=e118]
                - generic [ref=e120]: Section titled “When to use Aspire testing”
            - paragraph [ref=e121]: "Use Aspire testing when you want to:"
            - list [ref=e122]:
              - listitem [ref=e123]: Verify end-to-end functionality of your distributed application.
              - listitem [ref=e124]: Ensure interactions between multiple services and resources (such as databases) behave correctly in realistic conditions.
              - listitem [ref=e125]: Confirm data persistence and integration with real external dependencies, like a PostgreSQL database.
            - paragraph [ref=e126]:
              - text: If your goal is to test a single project in isolation, run components in-memory, or mock external dependencies, consider using
              - code [ref=e127]: WebApplicationFactory<T>
              - text: instead.
            - complementary "Note" [ref=e128]:
              - paragraph [ref=e129]:
                - img [ref=e130]
                - text: Note
              - paragraph [ref=e133]: Aspire tests run your application as separate processes, meaning you don’t have direct access to internal services or components from your test code. You can influence application behavior through environment variables or configuration settings, but internal state and services remain encapsulated within their respective processes.
